sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant S as Sender (Client)
  participant R as Receiver (Server :5001)
  Note over S,R: Each device owns a long-term Ed25519 identity key (IK)
  S->>R: TCP connect + magic "SFT1"
  Note left of S: generate ephemeral X25519 (eS, ES)<br/>nonce_c = 32 random bytes
  S->>R: ClientHello {name, device_id, IK_S.pub, ES, nonce_c}<br/>+ Sig_IK_S("HELLO|client"|body)
  Note right of R: verify signature with IK_S.pub
  Note right of R: generate ephemeral X25519 (eR, ER)
  R->>S: ServerHello {name, device_id, IK_R.pub, ER, nonce_s}<br/>+ Sig_IK_R("HELLO|server"|body|SHA256(ClientHello))
  Note left of S: verify signature + check fingerprint(IK_R)<br/>matches the discovered one (anti-MITM)
  Note over S,R: shared = X25519(e, E_peer)   transcript = SHA256(ClientHello || ServerHello)
  Note over S,R: HKDF-SHA256(shared, salt=transcript) -> K_c2s, K_s2c, IV_c2s, IV_s2c, SAS
  S->>R: AES-GCM{ FINISHED(transcript) }
  R->>S: AES-GCM{ FINISHED(transcript) }
  Note over S,R: Key confirmation OK - both users can compare the 6-digit SAS code
  S->>R: AES-GCM{ OFFER(name, size, SHA-256) }
  R-->>R: user sees SAS + fingerprint, accepts
  R->>S: AES-GCM{ ACCEPT }
  loop every 64 KiB chunk
    S->>R: AES-GCM{ DATA chunk_i }  nonce = IV || counter_i
  end
  S->>R: AES-GCM{ END }
  Note right of R: compare SHA-256 of received data<br/>with SHA-256 in OFFER
  R->>S: AES-GCM{ RESULT ok, sha256 }
